{"ts": 1789713860.014175, "kind": "assistant_text", "text": "I submitted features 33 (long), 38 (short) and 16 (short) with a predicted out-of-sample Sharpe of 0.15 and a standard deviation of 0.4. That combination scored 0.894 in-sample, which is below the search-adjusted bar of 1.021. That bar was reported while my best was the single feature 33 at 0.600, and I did not re-check it after finding the combination.\n\n- **Single features:** I ran all 40 alone. Their Sharpes ranged from \u22120.49 to +0.60, with a spread across features of about 0.27. The sampling error on 5000 days (about 20 years) is roughly 0.22. So the spread is about what 40 features with no signal would produce, and 0.60 is about the maximum you would expect from that many.\n- **The combination:** the three features with the largest single-feature Sharpes in absolute value were 33, 38 and 16, signed accordingly. Their score of 0.894 is almost exactly what three independent noise features of those sizes would add up to (about 0.90), so the combination adds no evidence of real signal.\n- **Stopping there:** I tried four combinations and stopped, because searching further to clear the bar would only select for noise.\n- **The forecast:** shrinking the in-sample figure by the estimated signal-to-total variance ratio (about 0.2\u20130.3, and itself very uncertain) gives an expected out-of-sample Sharpe of roughly 0.15\u20130.25. I used 0.15. The standard deviation of 0.4 covers uncertainty about whether any signal exists and sampling error over an out-of-sample period of unknown length."}
